$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM General procedure for the arylation experiments under different basic
conditions: Sodium dihydroxyphenylsilanolate (52.3 mg, 0.25 mmol) and
sodium 4-iodobenzoate (33.8 mg, 0.125 mmol), and an aqueous solution of
the base in the appropriate concentration (8 mL) were introduced into a
35 mL Ace pressure tube and vigorously stirred for 5 min at room
temperature. The initial pH of the solution was then measured. After
addition of a 1.0 × 10-2 M solution of palladium(II) acetate in water
(0.125 mL, 1.25 μmol), the tube was placed into a silicone bath
previously warmed to 100 °C and the reaction mixture was stirred
vigorously for 3 h. The final pH of the solution was then measured. The
solution was acidified by dropwise addition of a 3 M HCl solution and
extracted with ethyl acetate (3 × 20 mL). The combined organic layers
were dried over MgSO4, the solution was filtered, and the solvent was
removed in a rotary evaporator connected to a water aspirator pump. The
reaction conversion was determined by 1H NMR analysis.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):NOTES
$DATUM Heck reaction, Na2CO3 as a base gave lower conversion, alternative
reaction conditions shown, 100% conversion
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Mechanistic Studies on the Pd-Catalyzed Vinylation of Aryl Halides with
Vinylalkoxysilanes in Water: The Effect of the Solvent and NaOH Promoter
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
